00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:53.720
be my guest or I can try and find a way to distribute this afterwards.

244
00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:57.400
I mentioned

245
00:12:57.400 --> 00:13:01.280
that we put some time into creating a workshop around this.

246
00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:04.080
Sorry, did everyone manage to get a photo that wanted it on there?

247
00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:05.880
Yeah,

248
00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:07.720
we put a workshop around this because I think

249
00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:11.680
having a model itself is okay, but actually using it

250
00:13:11.680 --> 00:13:15.760
to drive conversations within the teams was the more valuable part of the process.

251
00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:20.000
And so we had this, this setup where we could go in, we'd help facilitate

252
00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:20.600
the first session.

253
00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:24.160
We talked through the different aspects of the model and what it meant.

254
00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:29.880
And then we'd we'd set the teams up with a regular period of every 3 or 4 weeks.

255
00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:31.720
They'd maybe run it again and give us some feedback

256
00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:34.440
on how they were finding themselves progressing over time.

257
00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:37.280
And the nice thing about this is because it was team specific.

258
00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:41.040
They'd find their own gaps, and they'd find unique ways that they could build

259
00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:44.040
new ideas into their work in practice on a day to day basis.

260
00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:46.840
And it's that

261
00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:50.120
tracking progress over time that really made the difference here,

262
00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:51.760
because we wanted to make sure that people

263
00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:54.080
were seeing value in the time that they were investing,

264
00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:56.000
but if they didn't have any kind of way of measuring it,

265
00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:58.760
this is where the model really helps out. They could actually see, oh yeah, right.

266
00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:00.240
We did try that thing.

267
00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:04.080
Now we're seeing ourselves embedding this process AI into our processes more.

268
00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:06.520
We're seeing more value in trying this prompting technique.

269
00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:08.720
Or we found this tool that we're going to use more.

270
00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:11.720
So they could they could track it.

271
00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:14.000
This estimate was really, really simple.

272
00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:18.200
If you want to run this yourselves, it's it's not rocket science at all.

273
00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:22.040
It's a very basic sort of kaizen retro format where we would

274
00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:26.000
talk through the descriptions, we would get people to vote in isolation.

275
00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:29.800
Something like Miro or another kind of online whiteboard

276
00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:33.120
is great for this sort of thing where you can just have anonymous voting.

277
00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:34.840
And then the key bit was the discussion.

278
00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:38.680
The 20 to 30 minutes often spiraled into 45 minutes plus,

279
00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:42.000
and then we would talk and share learnings with the community.

280
00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:45.800
And one of the things in meta that we use a lot is called workplace,

281
00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:47.920
which is essentially an internal version of Facebook.

282
00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:50.360
So every time anyone ran one of these assessments,

283
00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:53.120
they would post the results and they would share the insights they'd uncovered.

284
00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:55.080
And so it was a case of everybody else would then

285
00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:59.200
get visibility into what other teams were doing, raising up the network effect.

286
00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:03.720
One of the big things that came out from pretty much

287
00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:05.840
every single workshop was, can we trust the code?

288
00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:07.280
And it was one of the big reasons

289
00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:10.680
of the senior engineers was slightly hesitant about adopting

290
00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:13.000
AI tooling in the first place. They wanted to have control.

291
00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:14.880
They liked finessing what they were doing.

292
00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:17.560
They like to feel that they had the craft in hand.

293
00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:22.960
And that was great because it told us where we could focus on

294
00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:26.720
more cross-cutting efforts as a leadership group, a team that were looking to help

295
00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:28.160
the adoption curve.

296
00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:33.160
We could find ways to to unilaterally improve people's experiences.

297
00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:37.760
So on the back of things like we were, we were looking at testing

298
00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:39.480
and how we could increase our test coverage

299
00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:42.880
and reduce flakiness to just in comments before.

300
00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:48.480
Well, we were seeing that AI was great at hallucinating awful tests.

301
00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:50.400
It would make stuff that was really looking like

302
00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:52.080
it was doing something useful, but it really wasn't,

303
00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:54.400
and it was just increasing our overhead on CI.

304
00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:58.840
So we invested in an anti-test-slop initiative, where again, on CI,

305
00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:01.880
we could go and validate the changes that were being made in an autonomous

306
00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:05.160
way, using a different AI tool to judge the quality of the output.

307
00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:10.360
And these things are again, examples of the ground up culture that we have

308
00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:13.400
is that this thing started out in horizon, but the Test Slop

309
00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:16.440
initiative that we started and the tooling that was built

310
00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:20.080
was eventually adopted by infra and rolled out across the whole company.

311
00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:23.120
And I think to date, it still runs pretty much on every diff.

312
00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:26.480
That's got significant volume of AI generated tests in it.

313
00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:29.680
So as time

314
00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:33.880
progressed, the momentum built and this is when the other side of it.

315
00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:35.080
So we have the ground up bit.

316
00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:37.600
This is where the sort of top down aspect of support came in.

317
00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:42.640
So we leadership saw that there was a good thing happening here.

318
00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:47.520
And that's where we got the kind of support and push to make this broader.

319
00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:51.320
So we opened this, this whole idea up to more than our local teams.

320
00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:54.480
We push this out to the whole of the metaverse organization.

321
00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:57.560
And it was a way that we could

322
00:16:57.560 --> 00:17:01.920
we could start to scale this and help with the speed of adoption.

323
00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:06.800
And what we saw was there was quite a step change in our community

324
00:17:06.840 --> 00:17:07.800
just by these things.

325
00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:09.000
And this is where I think

326
00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:12.320
you start to get to a snowball effect, where you reach a critical mass and enough

327
00:17:12.320 --> 00:17:15.920
people know and enough people start sharing things out, that the adoption

328
00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:19.760
and the numbers just grow fairly organically and rapidly.

329
00:17:20.760 --> 00:17:22.840
So I've got some patterns of the things that we saw

330
00:17:22.840 --> 00:17:27.480
that were helping people in their their focus on improving their practice,

331
00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:31.200
and they kind of go through the maturity model in some respects as well.

332
00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:33.440
So we started off by saying,

333
00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:37.440
how can we think about test tests are always problematic.

334
00:17:37.480 --> 00:17:40.760
We know that we want to have more of them, the ones that we've got,

335
00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:44.200
especially when we're thinking about building a VR immersive experience.

336
00:17:44.200 --> 00:17:45.400
And you need to have headsets

337
00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:48.960
in a device lab, and we want to have entry and testing and things and multi users,

338
00:17:49.080 --> 00:17:50.960
they're hard to write in a hard to maintain.

339
00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:54.080
And they're not exactly the most fun thing for people to be fixing all the time.

340
00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:57.200
So how can we improve our test coverage.

341
00:17:57.200 --> 00:18:00.200
And is there a way that we can use agents more cleverly

342
00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:04.640
to find the best places where we could increase our test coverage?

343
00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:08.200
So one of the engineers, Balraj, he went off and did this.

344
00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:11.720
He identified some dashboards and some some data that we had some

345
00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:15.640
some tables that were telling us what files were uncovered,

346
00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:19.400
but then could also understand how those files were being changed over time.

347
00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:23.320
Were they hotspots for people making critical changes to the product?

348
00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:27.560
And then he taught the agent

349
00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:31.640
how to go and find that data, and how to assess it, and build up

350
00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:34.840
a priority list of files that we wanted to go in, write test coverage for.

351
00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:40.880
And in the end, I think this the time estimate here is quite significant.

352
00:18:40.920 --> 00:18:43.960
He applied this and he thinks in about three hours he achieved

353
00:18:43.960 --> 00:18:47.200
what he would have taken about half a week normally, which I think

354
00:18:48.200 --> 00:18:49.320
this is an early adopter.

355
00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:51.040
So it might be was a bit faster.

356
00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:52.960
He got there a bit faster than some other people would have done,

357
00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:55.320
but it showed the potential in this approach.

358
00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:55.720
And again,

359
00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:58.960
it was a repeatable pattern that we could think about rolling out more broadly.

360
00:18:58.960 --> 00:19:02.080
Because this these tables had data about lots

361
00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:05.280
and lots of files in the meta ecosystem, not just the horizon ones.

362
00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:09.120
And I think from this initial effort, nearly 60 diffs

363
00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:12.160
got merged.

364
00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:14.720
VR, like I mentioned,

365
00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:18.760
is quite a challenging world to build software for in some respects.

366
00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:23.600
And one of the challenges is that you we largely work

367
00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:27.400
on windows machines that are very, very powerful with very big GPUs.

368
00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:30.520
And so they're not necessarily the easiest things to rack up in a.

369
00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:36.840
In a server center and provide on demand to people.

370
00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:40.800
We tend to have 1 or 2 beefy machines per person.

371
00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:45.200
And so there's a lot of things that you can only do kind of single threaded.

372
00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:47.760
Refactoring in

373
00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:50.880
VR means that you're going to be spending a lot of time in your windows

374
00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:54.640
PC looking at some pretty gnarly low level code at times.

375
00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:55.960
And there's a lot of legacy patterns

376
00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:58.480
that we'd built up over the years that needed to be refactored out.

377
00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:00.560
And people tended to shy away from these things

378
00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:02.960
because there's a higher risk of breaking things.

379
00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:06.680
You know, these are the refactorings and not something that you would undertake

380
00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:08.960
for fun.

381
00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:13.040
But one of the engineers thought, okay, well, if I can teach the AI

382
00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:16.320
the way I would think about resolving these refactorings and then go

383
00:20:16.320 --> 00:20:20.080
and find patterns that are similar across the code base, what can we achieve?

384
00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:26.160
And again, roughly half the time spent in achieving his goal.

385
00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:30.520
But he went out and found multiple areas where this pattern could be reapplied

386
00:20:30.520 --> 00:20:31.600
and set the agents off

387
00:20:31.600 --> 00:20:34.600
in a kind of team pattern, so they would go and work in parallel.

388
00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:36.280
And then he was actually more as a reviewer

389
00:20:36.280 --> 00:20:38.600
and just being a critical architectural oversight,

390
00:20:38.600 --> 00:20:40.160
making sure that it was doing the right thing

391
00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:43.400
and not making any kind of gross miscalculations

392
00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:46.000
on how we actually wanted the system to look afterwards.

393
00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:48.880
And moving on from that, like I said,

394
00:20:48.880 --> 00:20:54.000
we had a challenge with VR in that you're kind of constrained to one machine.

395
00:20:54.440 --> 00:20:58.120
But one of the engineers who was thinking about this from the

396
00:20:58.160 --> 00:21:01.480
what is the what is the machine actually need to render and what what can we do

397
00:21:01.520 --> 00:21:05.920
that doesn't require us to have this massive PC with a huge GPU in it?

398
00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:10.640
If we provide better context to the models when they're doing work,

399
00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:14.400
would they be able to work in our more standardized on demand worlds?

400
00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:18.320
And the result of this was an MCP server

401
00:21:18.320 --> 00:21:22.320
that was a bridge between the data that backs Horizon World.

402
00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:25.600
So all of the things that explain how world state looks,

403
00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:26.960
how how things are built,

404
00:21:26.960 --> 00:21:30.040
the models, the kind of textures and everything that are in the worlds,

405
00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:32.800
it taught the models exactly what it needed to know

406
00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:36.240
about how horizon works, so they could go and work largely

407
00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:40.200
in isolation and in multiple on demand environments.

408
00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:44.080
And so that removes the whole windows PC barrier.

409
00:21:44.080 --> 00:21:47.800
And it meant that we could parallelize the work in a lot more effective way.

410
00:21:48.120 --> 00:21:50.840
And again, the AI was cleverer.

411
00:21:50.840 --> 00:21:54.840
It knew more about the the world that we were working in than it did before.

412
00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:56.800
So we got better outputs as well.

413
00:21:56.800 --> 00:21:59.600
So not only was it more efficient and we could parallelize more,

414
00:21:59.600 --> 00:22:02.320
we actually found that the quality of the work went up to.

415
00:22:04.120 --> 00:22:06.000
And then lastly,

416
00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:10.440
meta has a pretty great infrastructure for rolling out code mods.

417
00:22:10.440 --> 00:22:13.960
And the infrastructure teams have been experimenting with ways

418
00:22:13.960 --> 00:22:18.200
that we can integrate agents code mods into that platform.

419
00:22:18.280 --> 00:22:23.680
And as you saw with our 500 million lines of hack back in 2023,

420
00:22:23.720 --> 00:22:24.840
that has only got bigger

421
00:22:24.840 --> 00:22:28.040
since there were many, many places where they were going to be code

422
00:22:28.040 --> 00:22:31.080
quality issues that were just not worth the effort finding them,

423
00:22:31.240 --> 00:22:33.160
unless we could do it in an automated way.

424
00:22:33.160 --> 00:22:36.720
And that's where this automated code fixed pattern really came to the fore.

425
00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:41.840
So we could generate ways to find these issues, and we could provide

426
00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:46.000
rules and run books to the AI that look basically like skills or context files.

427
00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:50.240
And it would just churn constantly, just run, go, go,

428
00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:53.600
find a thing, fix it, promote a diff, get someone to review it.

429
00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:58.040
And 30 diffs generated autonomously is is a pretty low number.

430
00:22:58.080 --> 00:23:02.080
I'm fairly confident that that number is several orders of magnitude higher.

431
00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:05.000
Now, this was just at the start of the use of this,

432
00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:08.200
but this was a way that we found to automate code quality at scale.

433
00:23:08.200 --> 00:23:12.440
And again, it moved away from humans doing this in a sort of supervised way

434
00:23:12.440 --> 00:23:16.320
to a largely unsupervised review afterwards manner.

435
00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:21.360
So what was it behind these wins that kind of made them successful?

436
00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:24.720
Thinking about this helps us to look for more opportunities in the future.

437
00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:28.840
So being clear on what the problem definition was was the main thing.

438
00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:31.120
We weren't just sort of scatter gunning ideas and trying things.

439
00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:34.600
We'd had a clear vision that we wanted to focus on our engineering excellence.

440
00:23:34.840 --> 00:23:38.400
We tried to focus on shared tools where we could

441
00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:39.880
and things that were going to be

442
00:23:39.880 --> 00:23:43.320
unilaterally used across the company so we could benefit from cross-pollination

443
00:23:43.320 --> 00:23:43.800
of ideas.

444
00:23:43.800 --> 00:23:46.800
And one person improves the other people get the benefit.

445
00:23:47.000 --> 00:23:50.240
But we also try to focus on keeping it as a human oversight in a first place.

446
00:23:50.240 --> 00:23:53.360
If we want to far into the fully unsupervised agentic world,

447
00:23:53.360 --> 00:23:56.360
I think we were going to set ourselves up for failure.

448
00:23:56.520 --> 00:24:00.000
And then all the things that we know from years and years of agile work,

449
00:24:00.040 --> 00:24:04.640
iteration, learning, being open minded, sharing the approach

450
00:24:04.640 --> 00:24:07.680
that we get feedback and looking for other people's insights along the way.

451
00:24:07.720 --> 00:24:11.600
And all of this was available to us using commercial tools.

452
00:24:11.600 --> 00:24:15.160
It's not something that is restricted to a meta internal only.

453
00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:16.800
Thinking of the

454
00:24:16.800 --> 00:24:21.160
tools that we did use, I quite like to map them

455
00:24:21.360 --> 00:24:25.440
across the stock, because code is only one part of the story.

456
00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:27.960
And I think actually if we want to be AI-native,

457
00:24:27.960 --> 00:24:31.360
then we need to think about this more broadly across all aspects

458
00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:35.200
of what it takes to deliver software from idea to production.

459
00:24:35.280 --> 00:24:37.360
And we have a bunch of things.

460
00:24:37.360 --> 00:24:42.840
At the time that I was running this, this, this group were available to us.

461
00:24:42.840 --> 00:24:46.400
But I think the key thing that I sort of reflected on this,

462
00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:49.560
that actually made a difference was that underpinning all of these things

463
00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:54.080
and all these stages in the lifecycle was a strong investment in platforms

464
00:24:54.080 --> 00:24:58.160
and platform engineering, because a lot of the tools that you see on

465
00:24:58.160 --> 00:25:03.960
the screen here were hooked up to to existing platforms and had entry points

466
00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:05.880
that were allow them to be

467
00:25:05.880 --> 00:25:10.000
relatively quickly onboarded and scaled across all the engineers in the company.

468
00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:13.800
And so that isn't it isn't a trivial amount of effort.

469
00:25:13.800 --> 00:25:15.360
And building those platforms in the first place

470
00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:17.080
actually is still paying dividends.

471
00:25:17.080 --> 00:25:20.760
Even if we think about a world where AI is supposedly reducing the need

472
00:25:20.760 --> 00:25:23.840
for multiple people, I think we can focus on doing more,

473
00:25:23.840 --> 00:25:26.640
but we still need to have these underpinning things that allow us to do

474
00:25:26.640 --> 00:25:29.640
more collectively rather than just individually.

475
00:25:30.360 --> 00:25:33.480
And there's an item at the bottom there that's doing a lot of heavy

476
00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:36.240
lifting as well. So build your own tools using Confucius.

477
00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:39.760
Confucius Confucius is our platform where people can build

478
00:25:39.760 --> 00:25:42.760
their own agents and have them to be highly specific.

479
00:25:43.120 --> 00:25:48.160
After I gave this talk for the first time when I saw Simon in New York,

480
00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:51.480
I invested a whole bunch of time in creating agents

481
00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:55.120
to help us with maintenance mode for different products.

